WEBVTT

1
00:00:16.201 --> 00:00:18.842
afternoon someone requested to see me in Mrs.

2
00:00:18.902 --> 00:00:19.942
Fairfax's room.

3
00:00:21.043 --> 00:00:26.165
To my great surprise, it was Robert, the coachman from Gateshead and Bessie's husband.

4
00:00:27.225 --> 00:00:31.647
He was dressed in mourning clothes, and the hat he held was trimmed in black crepe.

5
00:00:32.728 --> 00:00:33.508
"How is Bessie?"

6
00:00:33.852 --> 00:00:34.793
I asked anxiously.

7
00:00:34.813 --> 00:00:37.594
"Is everyone well in the Reed family?

8
00:00:37.614 --> 00:00:39.875
I hope no one has died."

9
00:00:39.895 --> 00:00:46.119
"Bessie is well," he replied, "but John Reed died a week ago in London."

10
00:00:47.299 --> 00:00:49.741
"I heard from Bessie that he wasn't doing well."

11
00:00:49.761 --> 00:00:52.222
"He could do no worse, miss.

12
00:00:53.383 --> 00:00:55.404
He got into debt and landed in jail.

13
00:00:56.464 --> 00:01:00.747
His mother tried to help him, but as soon as he was set free, he returned to his

14
00:01:00.847 --> 00:01:02.808
old companions and habits."

15
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:09.344
John had recently asked his mother to turn over the family fortune to him, Robert continued.

16
00:01:11.145 --> 00:01:15.528
But she refused, her wealth having been reduced by his extravagance.

17
00:01:15.548 --> 00:01:20.131
"The next news was that he'd killed himself," said Robert.

18
00:01:21.312 --> 00:01:22.793
I was shocked by this report.

19
00:01:22.813 --> 00:01:25.435
"And how is his mother bearing up?"

20
00:01:25.455 --> 00:01:29.117
"The fear of poverty was already weakening her, miss.

21
00:01:30.278 --> 00:01:32.960
The information about his death brought on a stroke."

22
00:01:34.168 --> 00:01:35.229
Robert said that Mrs.

23
00:01:35.289 --> 00:01:39.392
Reed had gone days without speaking and then suddenly asked to see me.

24
00:01:39.412 --> 00:01:43.835
"I'd like to take you back with me tomorrow if you can get permission."

25
00:01:44.975 --> 00:01:48.177
I sent Robert off to the servants' hall while I went in search of Mr.

26
00:01:48.217 --> 00:01:53.461
Rochester. I found him playing billiards with Blanche Ingram and some of the guests.

27
00:01:54.622 --> 00:01:57.043
I approached him, but he had his back to me.

28
00:01:57.063 --> 00:02:00.846
"Does that person want you?"

29
00:02:01.306 --> 00:02:03.728
Miss Ingram asked, and he turned and saw me.

30
00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:09.524
Mr. Rochester guided me out into the hall, where I explained the situation.

31
00:02:09.544 --> 00:02:11.646
"How long will you stay?"

32
00:02:12.747 --> 00:02:14.709
He leaned against the billiards room door.

33
00:02:14.729 --> 00:02:17.512
"As short a time as possible, sir."

34
00:02:17.532 --> 00:02:20.194
"But you will come back.

35
00:02:21.275 --> 00:02:24.798
You won't be induced to take up permanent residence with her?"

36
00:02:24.818 --> 00:02:26.420
"Oh no!

37
00:02:27.001 --> 00:02:27.661
I'll return."

38
00:02:28.822 --> 00:02:30.304
I hesitated for a moment.

39
00:02:31.424 --> 00:02:36.245
"Mr. Rochester, I may as well mention another business matter while I have the opportunity."

40
00:02:36.265 --> 00:02:38.306
"A business matter?"

41
00:02:39.346 --> 00:02:41.687
"You've informed me that you're going to be married.

42
00:02:42.807 --> 00:02:45.588
In that case, sir, Adele should go to school."

43
00:02:45.608 --> 00:02:49.029
"To get her out of the way of my bride, I suppose.

44
00:02:50.169 --> 00:02:51.129
But where will you go?"

45
00:02:51.149 --> 00:02:53.810
"I'll advertise for other employment."

46
00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:57.371
"No," he said emphatically.

47
00:02:57.391 --> 00:03:01.172
"You must promise to trust this quest for employment to me."

48
00:03:02.224 --> 00:03:06.368
"I will if you'll promise that Adele and I will be safely out of the house before your

49
00:03:06.408 --> 00:03:07.249
bride enters it."

50
00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:12.133
He promised, and early the next morning, I journeyed to Gateshead.

51
00:03:13.174 --> 00:03:17.818
When I arrived, Bessie led me into the breakfast room, which looked just as it had the day

52
00:03:17.878 --> 00:03:18.499
I met Mr.

53
00:03:18.539 --> 00:03:23.843
Brocklehurst. After greeting me, Georgiana and Eliza ignored me.

54
00:03:24.884 --> 00:03:25.945
So I visited Mrs.

55
00:03:26.025 --> 00:03:28.748
Reed, but she was confused and incoherent.

56
00:03:29.832 --> 00:03:33.514
More than ten days elapsed before I had any real conversation with her.

57
00:03:34.654 --> 00:03:38.095
Meanwhile I got on as well as I could with Georgiana and Eliza.

58
00:03:38.115 --> 00:03:43.298
I had brought my drawing materials, so I wasn't at a loss for amusement.

59
00:03:44.518 --> 00:03:46.499
One morning I began sketching a face.

60
00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.180
At first I didn't know whose it was.

61
00:03:51.341 --> 00:03:57.043
But as I worked away with my soft black pencil, I soon recognized the dark hair and equally

62
00:03:57.103 --> 00:03:58.144
dark eyes of Mr.

63
00:03:58.204 --> 00:04:04.417
Rochester. I now had my friend's face to look at, so it didn't matter that the young ladies

64
00:04:04.457 --> 00:04:05.798
had turned their backs on me.

65
00:04:05.818 --> 00:04:08.718
"Is that a portrait of someone you know?"

66
00:04:09.219 --> 00:04:13.060
asked Eliza, who had suddenly approached.

67
00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:17.561
"No no I hurried to hide the drawing beneath some others.

68
00:04:18.741 --> 00:04:21.062
Georgiana also came over to look at my work.

69
00:04:22.282 --> 00:04:24.563
Both women seemed surprised at my skill.

70
00:04:25.623 --> 00:04:29.484
When I offered to draw their portraits, they each sat for a pencil sketch.

71
00:04:30.736 --> 00:04:33.137
Later Georgiana proposed a walk on the grounds.

72
00:04:34.298 --> 00:04:39.501
She was soon confiding in me about the brilliant winter she had spent in London, where she had

73
00:04:39.541 --> 00:04:41.642
met the nobleman who'd wanted to marry her.

74
00:04:43.583 --> 00:04:48.666
As the days passed, Eliza still spoke little; she evidently had no time to talk.

75
00:04:49.927 --> 00:04:57.431
After breakfast she divided her time into regular portions, and each hour had its allotted task: praying, sewing,

76
00:04:57.451 --> 00:04:59.572
writing, gardening.

77
00:05:00.756 --> 00:05:05.680
She told me one evening that she had managed to secure her own fortune and that when her

78
00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:08.983
mother died, she would retreat to a tranquil place.

79
00:05:10.364 --> 00:05:15.568
When not unburdening her heart to me, Georgiana spent most of her time lying on the sofa.

80
00:05:16.689 --> 00:05:22.273
It was clear to me that the sisters disliked each other and that Georgiana still blamed Eliza for

81
00:05:22.353 --> 00:05:24.615
ruining her chances of marrying the nobleman.

82
00:05:25.956 --> 00:05:30.340
One wet and windy afternoon, their arguing left Georgiana in tears.

83
00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:36.044
Eliza immediately busied herself with work, so I went upstairs to visit Mrs.

84
00:05:36.124 --> 00:05:43.450
Reed. At first she didn't recognize me, but she began to speak once she was assured that I

85
00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:45.992
was Jane Eyre and that we were alone.

86
00:05:46.012 --> 00:05:51.637
"I have twice done you wrong, which I regret now," she said.

87
00:05:51.657 --> 00:05:57.642
"Once was in breaking the promise I made to my husband to bring you up as my own

88
00:05:57.742 --> 00:06:05.009
child. The other She paused and seemed to be considering whether to continue.

89
00:06:06.169 --> 00:06:10.191
Finally she ordered me to remove a letter from a drawer in her dressing table.

90
00:06:10.211 --> 00:06:13.091
"Read it," she commanded.

91
00:06:14.272 --> 00:06:20.093
Madame, Will you have the goodness to send me the address of my niece, Jane Eyre, and to

92
00:06:20.153 --> 00:06:21.214
tell me how she is?

93
00:06:22.474 --> 00:06:25.195
I intend to invite her to live with me in Madeira.

94
00:06:26.315 --> 00:06:32.053
As I am a prosperous man with no family, I wish to adopt her during my life and

95
00:06:32.073 --> 00:06:34.234
bequeath her my estate at my death.

96
00:06:35.575 --> 00:06:43.458
Sincerely, John Eyre I was stunned to see that the letter was dated three years earlier.

97
00:06:43.478 --> 00:06:46.039
"Why did I never hear of this?"

98
00:06:46.059 --> 00:06:51.262
"Because I disliked you too thoroughly to ever help you reach prosperity.

99
00:06:52.382 --> 00:06:55.744
I could never forget the fury in which you declared your hatred of me.

100
00:06:57.204 --> 00:06:58.485
Oh, bring me some water!

101
00:06:59.025 --> 00:07:01.587
Hurry!" I fetched some water.

102
00:07:01.607 --> 00:07:04.589
"Forgive me my passionate language, Mrs.

103
00:07:04.649 --> 00:07:09.873
Reed. I was but a child then, and eight or nine years have now passed."

104
00:07:11.053 --> 00:07:16.197
"I could not forget your fury," she repeated, "and so I took my revenge.

105
00:07:17.398 --> 00:07:22.221
I told John Eyre that you'd died of typhus fever at Lowood."

106
00:07:22.241 --> 00:07:26.524
I tried to tell her that I'd forgiven her and wanted to be reconciled with her.

107
00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:29.121
But my aunt would not listen.

108
00:07:30.262 --> 00:07:35.445
Finally I bent down so she could kiss my cheek, but she said I oppressed her by leaning

109
00:07:35.525 --> 00:07:36.146
over the bed.

110
00:07:37.246 --> 00:07:38.988
That night she died.
